<!-- @before 9. Major Concerns :: guide -->
### A local prompt is not a server action grant

The canonical source now separates a local UI gate from action-bound proof. For a protected server action, follow its challenge, enrolled-key proof and short-lived action grant contract. A prompt result or local timestamp alone cannot authorize that operation. The preserved “Major Concerns” storage-key suggestion concerns local key use; it does not replace this server contract. See [Android biometric cryptographic authentication](https://developer.android.com/identity/sign-in/biometric-auth) and the linked canonical Security chapters.

<!-- @after 12. Further Reading :: recap -->
### Say these in the interview

- **Bind the browser result to its login attempt.** Use Authorization Code with PKCE in an external user agent and validate the registered redirect, response state and provider contract before exchange. The chapter assumes a verified HTTPS App Link.
- **Capture one login’s objects.** A new login gets a new vault and refresher; invalidation permanently rejects replacement in the old vault.
- **Serialize and re-read.** A shared Mutex prevents overlapping refresh owners in this process; re-reading T2 avoids another request for failed T1.
- **Bound started rotation and commit.** Caller cancellation waits for that bounded work, then propagates. This cannot make the IdP and local disk atomic across process death.
- **Retry only the declared request class.** The source retries a bodyless read once after a 401; writes remain caller-owned. Only a confirmed terminal credential rejection retires the captured login; transient failures retain its tokens.
- **Authorize sensitive server actions on the server.** Local biometric UI state is not action-bound authorization. Use the canonical enrolled-key and step-up grant contract when required.
